WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:05.973
SPEAKER: Heya.

00:00:05.973 --> 00:00:08.640
Today, we're going to talk about
how to build a custom sentiment

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.990
analysis model with Google
Cloud AutoML Natural Language.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.360
If you've played with the
Natural Language API before,

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.027
you might have seen
that it can tell you

00:00:17.027 --> 00:00:18.480
the sentiment of
a block of text,

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.880
like whether a tweet is mostly
positive or mostly negative.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.882
But what about for text with
domain-specific language?

00:00:24.882 --> 00:00:26.340
For example, let's
say we're trying

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.280
to classify the sentiment
of a tweet like, Delta, I

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.542
was waiting on the
tarmac for two hours.

00:00:31.542 --> 00:00:33.000
That's a made-up
tweet, by the way.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.770
Sorry, Delta.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.300
An out of the box
sentiment model

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.140
would tell us this
tweet is neutral.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.182
It doesn't realize that
being stuck on the runway

00:00:40.182 --> 00:00:41.610
is a bad thing.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.040
For this, we'll use AutoML
Natural Language to train

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.720
a custom sentiment model.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.380
To do that, we'll need
a labeled data set.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.130
Today, we'll build a
model that identifies

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.890
the sentiment of tweets about
airlines using a data set built

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.230
by CrowdFlower.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.120
To prepare our data
set, all we have to do

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.820
is save the tweet with
our labels in a CSV.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.290
The leftmost column
indicates what

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.290
we should use a given example
for, like testing, training,

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:05.700
or validation.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.950
The label on the right are
the tweet sentiment scores,

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.700
which, in our data set, have
been labeled by a human being.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.810
0 is very negative,
1 is intermediate,

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.270
and 2 is very positive.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.190
Within the AutoML
Natural Language tool,

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:20.340
we'll choose to launch
an AutoML Sentiment app.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.490
Next, we'll import our data
by clicking Add Data Set.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:26.767
Here, we want to choose
Sentiment Analysis.

00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:28.350
We can upload the
data by importing it

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.510
as a CSV directly
or as a ZIP file,

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.270
but I've already uploaded my
CSV to a Google Cloud Storage

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.060
bucket, so I'm just going
to import it that way.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.920
Down here by
Sentiment Scale, we'll

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.810
tell AutoML the range
of sentiment scores

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.640
to expect in the data set.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.100
Since our scores go from
0, the most negative,

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.320
to 2, the most
positive, we'll select 2

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.387
as the maximum sentiment score.

00:01:48.387 --> 00:01:49.470
Now the data is importing.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.750
This could take a few
minutes to finish.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:56.012
Once data is imported, we'll see
it here in the Text Items tab.

00:01:56.012 --> 00:01:58.470
We can click into an example
and make sure it has the right

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:02.003
sentiment score, or
change it if it doesn't.

00:02:02.003 --> 00:02:04.170
Notice, we also have the
counts of how many examples

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.180
we have for each
label on the left.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.289
We'll need at least 100 examples
per label to start training.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:12.390
We can begin training a
model by navigating over

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.240
to the Train tab and
clicking Start Training.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.210
Training a model can take up
to three hours, so hold tight.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.408
You'll get an email
when it's done.

00:02:20.408 --> 00:02:22.950
When training's finished, we'll
be able to see our model here

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.520
in the Train tab.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.410
We can see how well it did by
clicking See Full Evaluation.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.240
Here in the Evaluate
panel, we can click in

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.310
to see the performance
of our model.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.830
The metrics we care about
are precision and recall.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:36.420
Up top, we can see
both, but if we

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.190
click into a single
sentiment label,

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:41.280
we can see it broken
down for each category.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.080
Precision tells
us, of the tweets

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.680
that our model did label
0, how many really were 0?

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.300
Recall, on the other
hand, tells us,

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.940
of all the entire tweets in the
data set, what percent of those

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.220
that should have been labeled
0 did our model actually

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.320
end up labeling 0?

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.801
Another useful tool is
the confusion matrix.

00:02:57.801 --> 00:03:00.000
It tells us how the real
labels of the data set

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.490
compare to the labels
that our model predicted.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:05.790
For example, 91.3% of the
time, the model accurately

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.710
predicted that tweets
with the label 0

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:11.580
should have had label 0,
but 26.7% of the time,

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.740
it also thought that
tweets with label 1--

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:15.030
those that are just neutral--

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:16.770
should be labeled 0.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.240
If we click on one
of these boxes,

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.850
we see examples of what the
model correctly and incorrectly

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.300
labeled.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:25.470
In the Predict tab, we can start
actually using our model hosted

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:26.552
by Google.

00:03:26.552 --> 00:03:29.010
We just have to think of some
positive and negative airline

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:32.110
tweets to type into this box.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:33.210
And there we go.

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:36.290
AutoML Natural Language's custom
sentiment analysis feature

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:39.000
knows that losing your
luggage is not fun.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:39.840
That's all for now.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.100
Have fun modeling.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

